# Pillow's Targa RLE decoder walks the input with one cursor and two byte
# counters. `ImagingTgaRleDecode` in src/libImaging/TgaRleDecode.c receives
# the compressed scan line in `buf` with its length in `bytes`, sets
# `ptr = buf`, and the outer `for (;;)` exits when `bytes < 1` -- so `bytes`
# is the count of bytes that still sit behind `ptr`. Every consumption in the
# outer loop keeps that relation: the run path does `ptr += 1 + depth;
# bytes -= 1 + depth;` and the literal path `ptr += 1 + n; bytes -= 1 + n;`.
# The literal path can hand a remainder to the inner cross-scan-line loop
# through `extra_bytes`, and that loop's own consumption keeps only its own
# counter: `memcpy(state->buffer + state->x, ptr, n); ptr += n;
# extra_bytes -= n;`. The `bytes` that bounds the whole call never shrank by
# `n`, so a truncated scan line left the outer loop believing data remained
# and reading past the end of `buf` (out-of-bounds read, CWE-125/CWE-835;
# the added cross_scan_line_truncated.tga fixture is the regressor). Fix
# c846cc881e ("When reading past the end of a scan line, reduce bytes left")
# adds the single line `bytes -= n;`, so the third consumption site decrements
# both counters over the cursor.
#
# ## The miss is counter-set coherence, and it is loop-carried
#
# The defect is one consumption site that advances a cursor while decrementing
# only one of the two counters the function itself establishes over that
# cursor. Nothing in the vocabulary asks "does every consumed chunk decrement
# every bound that bounds it": which counter a consumption belongs to is a
# relation between the cursor and the resource behind it, and it is carried
# across loop iterations, not stated at the site.
#
# ## Re-measured at the current tip
#
# Three `--cache off` runs per revision (timeout 180, --max-ram 6GB) over
# src/libImaging/TgaRleDecode.c: 5 findings at the parent and 5 at the fix,
# identical on every run -- VYQL-MEM-020 at function scope,
# VYQL-MEM-010 at the shuffle index, and VYQL-MEM-016 on each memcpy
# (:63, :81 and the vulnerable :121), all memory heuristics that fire on the
# guarded copies and say nothing about the stale bound. The `-flags only` view
# reports 30 flags per revision whose only difference is the one-line shift.
# Whole-tree `src/` scans: 5569 findings at the parent and 5569 at the fix,
# stable across all six runs, with identical per-rule multisets. So no rule
# discriminates, and the zero on the defect is real rather than a `-flags`
# replacement misread.
#
# The two later-shipped items in the nearest families do not reach it.
# VYQL-DOS-050 (`code.LoopRestartWithoutProgressCheck`, the loop-progress
# umbrella) is a JavaScript frontend fact over a *restart*: a `continue` whose
# dominating path passes no progress step for the variables the loop's exit
# reads. This loop has no restart -- the inner loop breaks on `extra_bytes`
# and `state->x`, and it advances both -- and the shape here is C, where that
# fact is not emitted at all. VYQL-DOS-029 (`code.NumericParserMissingProgressCheck`)
# does have a C feeder, but it is the fio_atol/fio_atof spelling of one
# library, not a construct predicate. VYQL-MEM-071 asks whether a
# destination's capacity is consulted; here it is, at `state->x + n >
# state->bytes` and in the inner loop's own clamp, and the defect is on the
# source side.
#
# ## The syntactic form of the question was measured and is not shippable
#
# The one relation the syntax can carry -- a cursor advanced at a site where a
# counter is decremented, while the same function advances the same cursor at
# another site decrementing a different counter -- was counted over five real
# C trees (1886 files: gpac, grpc, hermes, httpd, ImageMagick): 15 hits, every
# one of them correct code -- quicksort's `pi`/`pj` cursors, parser main loops
# that advance a data cursor over `data_size` while a block loop advances it
# over `desc_len`/`nal_size`/`length` (mpegts.c, decrypt_cenc_isma.c,
# meta.c's 8BIM walker), image kernels walking `p`/`q`/`r`, and dtoa's
# exponent scalings. The pattern of two counters over one cursor with
# different accounting sites is how ordinary parsers are written, and whether
# the outer bound must absorb the inner consumption is exactly what the
# syntax cannot see. A fact that counted it would report roughly one correct
# function per 125 files to reach this one.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1576: the cross-scan-line copy leaves the outer byte bound stale"
  lang c
  file src/libImaging/TgaRleDecode.c
  reject VYQL-DOS-029
  reject VYQL-DOS-050
  code
  ```
  #include <string.h>
  #include <stdint.h>

  typedef struct {
      uint8_t *buffer;
      int x;
      int bytes;
  } ImagingCodecState;

  int ImagingTgaRleDecode(uint8_t *im, ImagingCodecState *state, uint8_t *buf,
                          int bytes)
  {
      int n, depth;
      uint8_t *ptr;
      int extra_bytes = 0;

      ptr = buf;
      depth = 4;

      for (;;) {
          if (bytes < 1) {
              return ptr - buf;
          }

          n = depth * ((ptr[0] & 0x7f) + 1);
          if (ptr[0] & 0x80) {
              if (bytes < 1 + depth) {
                  break;
              }

              memset(state->buffer + state->x, ptr[1], n);

              ptr += 1 + depth;
              bytes -= 1 + depth;
          } else {
              if (bytes < 1 + n) {
                  break;
              }

              if (state->x + n > state->bytes) {
                  extra_bytes = n;
                  n = state->bytes - state->x;
                  extra_bytes -= n;
              }

              memcpy(state->buffer + state->x, ptr + 1, n);

              ptr += 1 + n;
              bytes -= 1 + n;
          }

          for (;;) {
              state->x += n;

              if (state->x >= state->bytes) {
                  state->x = 0;
              }

              if (extra_bytes == 0) {
                  break;
              }

              if (state->x > 0) {
                  break;
              }

              if (extra_bytes >= state->bytes) {
                  n = state->bytes;
              } else {
                  n = extra_bytes;
              }
              memcpy(state->buffer + state->x, ptr, n);
              ptr += n;
              extra_bytes -= n;
          }
      }

      return ptr - buf;
  }
  ```

